e fact that R.B. was able to recover these functions suggests that\n717:a sense of personal ownership of ongoing experiences that occurred\n725:3.2 The sense of identity and quasi-memory\n729:discussions of personal identity have been dominated by psychological\n731:identity (but see Olson 1997; Shoemaker forthcoming). On a simple\n745:identity. And if memory presupposes personal identity, then trying\n746:to give an account of identity in terms of memory seems hopeless.\n755:personal identity (as opposed to, say, biological accounts; e.g. Olson\n761:cularity objection draws on the notion of quasi-memory. Originally\n762:proposed by Shoemaker (1970), Parfit defines quasi-memory as\n764:  I have an accurate quasi-memory of a past experience if:\n770:definition of quasi-memory, a Lockean can maintain, without circu-\n771:larity, that quasi-memory partly constitutes personal identity.\n772:   Advocates of psychological-continuity accounts of personal identity\n773:tend to take the quasi-memory account as solving the problem of cir-\n778:personal identity without presupposing identity (1990, p. 79).\n780:quasi-memory is supposed to evade the circularity objection by char-\n782:identity ’ (Schechtman 1990, p. 79). Schechtman charts the relation\n797:   and to take it (incorrectly) to be one’s own. To have a quasi-memory is to\n801:memories, and since having a quasi-memory leaves open whose\n802:memory it is, quasi-memory does not presuppose personal identity.\n811:quasi-memory, the state will either be a delusion or it will presuppose\n812:personal identity.6 As a result, no candidate quasi-memory will work\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
